---
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';

const stages = [
  ['Collection', 'A field officer walks the boundary in TaroWorks. The form rejects invalid shapes and missing fields on the spot.'],
  ['Conversion', 'The TaroWorks export is converted to GeoJSON, Shapefile and KML for office use.'],
  ['Office review', 'GIS checks size, shape, overlaps and position against reference layers, and works through a fixed order of fixes.'],
  ['Retake', 'If the boundary has to change, the officer picks one reason from a fixed list. The old polygon is kept.'],
  ['Approval and overwrite', 'Field staff confirm the polygon matches the ground. Only then does it replace the old one, which stays in the history.'],
];
const retake = [
  'Officer selects a retake reason during a farm visit',
  'New boundary captured; previous polygon retained',
  'System compares old and new area and tests the change against the reason',
  'MEL reviews any flag on site',
  'GIS validates the geometry',
  'Field staff approve, or send it back for another retake',
  'Approved polygon becomes the record; the prior version stays in the history',
];
---

<CaseStudy
  slug="polygon-lifecycle"
  facts={[
    ['Where', 'Trees for the Future, Kenya'],
    ['Tools', 'TaroWorks, Salesforce, ArcGIS Pro, KML for field review'],
    ['Standards', 'Verra VM0042 and VM0047'],
  ]}
>
  <section class="split">
    <h2 class="label">Problem</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        A farm polygon is the unit of carbon accounting. If a boundary changes with no record of why, the area changes,
        the additionality check changes and the audit trail breaks.
      </p>
      <p>
        Polygons were collected, converted, reviewed and retaken by different people, and there was no single written
        standard that covered the whole chain.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="split">
    <h2 class="label">What I did</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I wrote a master SOP that follows a polygon through five stages, with roles, known issues and a register of
        open questions. One rule governs it: nothing is overwritten before approval, and every change carries exactly
        one stated reason.
      </p>
      <ol class="stages">
        {
          stages.map(([name, text]) => (
            <li>
              <strong>{name}</strong>
              <span>{text}</span>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>
      <p>
        Office review follows a quality standard aligned with VM0047: size thresholds, shape and topology checks,
        exclusion of forest reserves, water bodies, protected areas and roads, position against field reference points,
        and exclusion of polygons with more than 10 percent existing tree cover.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="split">
    <h2 class="label">The retake loop</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <ol class="steps">
        {retake.map((step) => <li>{step}</li>)}
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="split">
    <h2 class="label">Result</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Review identified 829 polygons that needed retaking, for three reasons: placement errors, too much existing
        tree cover, and relocation. Programme managers and field officers reviewed the cleaned polygons and confirmed
        each against the ground before anything was overwritten. Field teams received updated KML files at each stage.
      </p>
      <p>
        All 35 project units went from no GIS to meeting the GIS requirements of VM0042 and VM0047, with documentation
        that was audit-ready under VM0042.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="split">
    <h2 class="label">Next steps</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The SOP was written for Kenya as a model that can extend to Uganda and Tanzania. The route for sending a
        rejected polygon back to the field still needs a proper interface.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</CaseStudy>

<style>
  .stages,
  .steps {
    display: grid;
    counter-reset: n;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .stages li,
  .steps li {
    counter-increment: n;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 2.5rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 0.25rem 0;
    padding-block: 0.9rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .stages li::before,
  .steps li::before {
    content: counter(n, decimal-leading-zero);
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--accent);
    padding-top: 0.3em;
  }

  .stages li::before {
    grid-row: span 2;
  }

  .stages span {
    grid-column: 2;
    font-size: 1rem;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
</style>
